  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 90,
    "conclusion": "The evidence supports a real Site A speed advantage, but not the claim that the new scanner is its primary driver. Site A was already about 3% faster before rollout; the scanner’s randomized effect was about a 4% reduction in picking time (95% CI: 1%–7%), which likely explains part—but not all—of the raw 11% post-rollout gap. The cross-site timestamp offset should be retained as a measurement confound and corrected, although close within-site agreement and 94% task-classification agreement argue against general measurement unreliability. Differences in order size, product mix, shift, and staffing pressure should be rejected as complete explanations because Site A remained 5%–9% faster after stratification, but operational complexity should be retained as an effect modifier because the advantage was largest for high-complexity orders. The remaining gap is most consistent with a persistent pre-existing site advantage plus scanner gains and other unmeasured site-specific processes."
  },
